<section class="reference-section" id="rama-exile"><div class="reference-section-heading"><h2>Rama’s exile</h2><a href="/rama-exile.html">Return to story</a></div><div class="reference-content"><p><strong>Valmiki Ramayana · Ayodhya Kanda.</strong> This is an original condensed retelling, ending at the first dwelling at Chitrakuta. The reference trail uses the Sanskrit text, word meanings and English translation presented by the Valmiki Ramayan website, with the Sanskrit Documents mirror for the early chapters. Its translation credits include K. M. K. Murthy. Chapter numbering follows those pages; extended dialogue and rituals are condensed.</p><ol><li><a href="https://sanskritdocuments.org/sites/valmikiramayan/ayodhya/sarga2/ayodhyaroman2.htm">Ayodhya Kanda · Sarga 2</a><small>The proposed royal succession and the assembly’s approval.</small></li><li><a href="https://sanskritdocuments.org/sites/valmikiramayan/ayodhya/sarga9/ayodhyaroman9.htm">Ayodhya Kanda · Sarga 9</a><small>Manthara’s counsel and the remembered boons.</small></li><li><a href="https://sanskritdocuments.org/sites/valmikiramayan/ayodhya/sarga18/ayodhyaroman18.htm">Ayodhya Kanda · Sarga 18</a><small>Kaikeyi tells Rama the terms: Bharata’s consecration and fourteen years in the forest.</small></li><li><a href="https://sanskritdocuments.org/sites/valmikiramayan/ayodhya/sarga19/ayodhyaroman19.htm">Ayodhya Kanda · Sarga 19</a><small>Rama accepts and asks that Bharata be summoned.</small></li><li><a href="https://www.valmikiramayan.net/utf8/ayodhya/sarga30/ayodhyaitrans30.htm">Ayodhya Kanda · Sarga 30</a><small>Sita’s insistence and Rama’s agreement.</small></li><li><a href="https://www.valmikiramayan.net/utf8/ayodhya/sarga31/ayodhyaitrans31.htm">Ayodhya Kanda · Sarga 31</a><small>Lakshmana’s request to accompany them.</small></li><li><a href="https://www.valmikiramayan.net/utf8/ayodhya/sarga40/ayodhyaitrans40.htm">Ayodhya Kanda · Sarga 40</a><small>Family farewells, the chariot and the grieving citizens.</small></li><li><a href="https://www.valmikiramayan.net/utf8/ayodhya/sarga46/ayodhyaitrans46.htm">Ayodhya Kanda · Sarga 46</a><small>The first night at the Tamasa and departure while the citizens sleep.</small></li><li><a href="https://www.valmikiramayan.net/utf8/ayodhya/sarga52/ayodhyaitrans52.htm">Ayodhya Kanda · Sarga 52</a><small>Sumantra’s return, matted hair, the Ganga crossing and Sita’s prayers.</small></li><li><a href="https://www.valmikiramayan.net/utf8/ayodhya/sarga54/ayodhyaitrans54.htm">Ayodhya Kanda · Sarga 54</a><small>Bharadwaja’s hospitality and recommendation of Chitrakuta.</small></li><li><a href="https://www.valmikiramayan.net/utf8/ayodhya/sarga56/ayodhyaitrans56.htm">Ayodhya Kanda · Sarga 56</a><small>The forest landscape, dwelling and entry rites.</small></li></ol><p>Sita’s and Lakshmana’s choices remain explicit; Bharata is not presented as the instigator of the exile. Later encounters and the return to Ayodhya fall outside this reader. Other Ramayana traditions have their own emphases and are not silently merged here.</p><p>The reflection on changing expectations is editorial. Both new paintings are AI-generated devotional interpretations, each used once. Their architecture, forest and clothing are artistic settings rather than historical reconstructions.</p></div></section>
